6. Supporting More Meaningful Safety Training
Workplace safety training should reflect the actual risks employees face.

A general induction may be suitable for introducing site rules, but it may not adequately prepare someone for a specialized high-risk task.

AI-assisted training systems may help organize learning content, identify knowledge gaps, or recommend relevant training material based on roles and activities.

For example, a contractor involved in confined space work may require different training from a warehouse forklift operator.

AI can help coordinate information.

Competency must still be demonstrated through appropriate training, assessment, practical experience, and authorization.

Completing an online module does not automatically establish competence.

7. Giving Leadership Better Visibility Into Critical Risks
Senior management often receives safety dashboards showing incident rates, inspection totals, audit scores, and overdue actions.

These indicators can be useful, but they do not always reveal whether serious hazards are adequately controlled.

A more effective dashboard should help leaders understand:

Which high-consequence hazards are present

Whether critical controls have been verified

Where significant corrective actions remain open

Which activities have repeated safety deficiencies

Whether contractor performance requires intervention

Where environmental or occupational health risks are increasing

AI-assisted analysis can help summarize complex information and identify patterns.

Management must still examine the underlying evidence and decide what action is required.

