Can Artificial Intelligence Prevent Workplace Accidents?
AI can help organizations identify potential risks earlier, improve information analysis, and support preventive decisions. But it cannot guarantee that accidents will be prevented.

An AI model may recognize a pattern associated with previous incidents.

That does not mean the model can accurately predict when or where the next incident will occur.

Accidents often involve changing conditions, human interactions, equipment failures, organizational decisions, and combinations of factors that may not be fully represented in historical data.

For this reason, organizations should avoid treating AI risk scores as certainty.

The more practical objective is to use AI to support earlier investigation and stronger preventive action.

Success should be evaluated through evidence of better risk control, not simply the number of AI alerts generated.

The Biggest Risk: Overconfidence in AI
As AI becomes more common in EHS software, organizations must guard against a new type of operational risk: assuming that an intelligent system is always correct.

AI applications can produce false positives, miss important conditions, misinterpret information, or reflect weaknesses in their training data.

An automated summary may overlook a critical detail.

A computer vision model may misidentify an object.

A risk recommendation may be inappropriate for a particular work environment.

These limitations matter because workplace safety decisions can have serious consequences.

Responsible implementation requires clear boundaries.

Organizations should establish:

Defined use cases and intended outcomes.

Appropriate validation before operational deployment.

Competent human review of safety-critical outputs.

Clear ownership of decisions and corrective actions.

Data protection and worker privacy safeguards.

Monitoring for errors, performance drift and unintended consequences.

A process for challenging, correcting or rejecting AI recommendations.

AI should never become an excuse to weaken supervision, reduce necessary field verification, or transfer accountability away from responsible personnel.

What Human Expertise Still Does Better
Technology can process information at scale, but experienced safety professionals contribute capabilities that are difficult to reproduce reliably.

A safety officer may recognize tension between contractors that suggests communication problems.

An experienced engineer may notice that a maintenance method is technically unsuitable even when the documentation appears complete.

A supervisor may understand that a worker is hesitant to report a hazard because of production pressure.

A site leader may recognize that repeated shortcuts indicate a deeper problem with planning and resources.

These situations require context, empathy, judgment, and leadership.

They also demonstrate why safety culture cannot be automated.

A workplace where employees fear reporting concerns will not become safer simply because it installs AI software.

Trust, openness, competence, and management commitment remain essential.

